{
  "schema_version": 2,
  "story": "Willow was a young tree in a busy courtyard. Her long branches brushed the stones, and children rested beneath her leaves. She wished she could do something useful instead of simply standing in the same place.\n\nOne windy morning, a tiny nest tumbled beside her roots. Two sparrows circled above it, chirping anxiously. Willow could not walk or lift her branches high, but she could bend them low enough to help.\n\nShe lowered her softest branch and curled its leaves into a cradle. The sparrows nudged their nest inside. Willow held it carefully, feeling proud as the little family settled among the green leaves above the bench.\n\nThen rain began to fall. Her lower leaves were too thin to keep the nest dry, and water dripped through. Willow worried she had promised more than she could give. The sparrows huddled close beneath her bending branch.\n\nWillow remembered the little hollow in her trunk. She bent the cradle closer and closer until the sparrows could hop inside. The nest fitted perfectly, protected from the rain by sturdy wood and a curtain of leaves.\n\nBy morning, the sparrows were singing. Children gathered beneath Willow’s branches, listening to the new song. Willow stood in her usual place, but it no longer felt ordinary. Being steady had given someone a home.",
